 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Configuration",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The two diodes are in series between pin 1 and pin 2: current flows forward from pin 1 through diode 1 to pin 3, and from pin 3 through diode 2 to pin 2."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current with both diodes in use",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Continuous forward current is 215 mA per diode when only one conducts and drops to 125 mA per diode when both are loaded. The 250 mW dissipation and 500 K/W junction-to-ambient figures are for one diode loaded on an FR4 board with single-sided tin-plated copper and the standard footprint."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge capability",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The single-pulse forward current rating falls with pulse length: about 4 A for 1 µs, near 1 A at 1 ms and below 1 A towards 10 ms, for square pulses starting from a 25 °C junction."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage against temperature",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Typical reverse current at 80 V rises from tens of picoamps at -40 °C to around 10 nA at 25 °C, about 1 µA at 85 °C and over 20 µA at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitance against bias",
   "page": 5,
